{"result":"*******************************************************************************\n Revised: Feb 22, 2019              Kepler (spacecraft)                    -227\n                                  http://kepler.nasa.gov/\n\n NOTE:  Mission completed 2018-Oct-30 due to depletion of fuel. The final\n        trajectory, with predictions through early 2201, are provided here. \n        No further updates are expected.\n\n        During its 9 years of operation, Kepler reported detections of \n        2662 planets.\n\n Launched:  2009-Mar-7 03:49 UTC\n            7925-10L (Delta II) launch to Earth-trailing heliocentric orbit\n\n BACKGROUND:\n   Monitor stars for changes in brightness due to objects several thousand\n   km or larger crossing the disk of the star. Search will include all orbital\n   periods less than 2 years for all spectral star types.\n\n   On August 15, 2013, NASA reported attempts to repair two failed reaction\n   wheels (used to control spacecraft orientation) were unsuccessful. Options \n   are being considered to restructure the mission goals using the remaining\n   two wheels, combined with thrusters.\n \n OBJECTIVES:\n   1. Determine the percentage of large objects there are in or near the \n       habitable zone of a wide variety of stars;\n   2. Determine the distribution of sizes and shapes of the orbits of these \n        objects;\n   3. Estimate how many planets there are in multiple-star systems;\n   4. Determine the variety of orbit sizes and planet reflectivities, sizes,\n        masses and densities of short-period giant planets;\n   5. Identify additional members of each discovered planetary system using\n        other techniques; and\n   6. Determine the properties of those stars that harbor planetary systems. \n\n MISSION:\n   * Continuously point at a single star field in Cygnus-Lyra region \n      (except during Ka-band downlink).\n   * Roll the spacecraft 90 degrees about the line-of-sight every 3 months to \n      maintain Sun on solar arrays and radiator pointed to deep space.\n   * Monitor 100,000 main-sequence stars for planets\n   * Mission lifetime of 3.5 years extendable to at least 6 years\n   * Project management (development) at Jet Propulsion Laboratory\n   * Scientific Operations Center and Project management (operations) at \n      NASA-Ames Research Center\n   * Flight segment design & fabrication at Ball Aerospace & Technologies Corp.\n   * Mission Operations Center at Laboratory for Atmospheric and Space Physics\n      (LASP) University of Colorado\n   * Data Management Center at Space Telescope Science Institute\n   * Deep Space Network telemetry\n   * Routine contact\n      X-band contact twice a week for commanding, health and status\n      Ka-band contact once a month for science data downlink\n\n SPACECRAFT SYSTEMS:\n   Spacebased Photometer: 0.95-m aperture\n   Primary mirror       : 1.4 meter diameter Schmidt, 85% light weighted\n   Detectors            : 95 mega pixels (42 CCDs with 2200x1024 pixels)\n   Bandpass             : 430-890 nm FWHM\n   Dynamic range        : 9th to 16th magnitude stars\n   Fine guidance sensors: 4 CCDs located on science focal plane\n   Attitude stability   : <9 milli-arcsec, 3 sigma over 15 minutes.\n   Avionics             : Fully block redundant\n   Science data storage : >60 days\n   Uplink X-band        : 7.8125 bps to 2 kbps\n   Downlink X-band      : 10 bps to 16 kbps\n   Downlink Ka-band     : Up to 4.33125 Mbps\n\n   Flight segment and instrument mass: 1071 kg, maximum expected (10/06)\n   Flight segment and instrument power: 771 W , maximum expected (10/06)\n\n   Photometric one-sigma noise performance:\n     Total noise with solar-like stellar variability and photon\n     Shot noise for an mv=12 star: < 2x10-5\n   No mechanisms other than a one-time ejectable cover and three focus \n    mechanisms for the primary mirror.\n\n ORBIT:\n   Earth-trailing heliocentric orbit with a period of 372.5 days\n   Spacecraft slowly drifts away from the Earth to a distance of 0.5 AU \n    (worst case) after four years. \n   Advantage of this orbit is very-low disturbing torque on spacecraft, \n     producing very stable pointing attitude. \n\n  Post-launch trajectory (from JPL navigation; prediction after 2018-Oct-30):\n  Trajectory name                           Start               Stop\n  ----------------------------        -----------------   -----------------\n  KEPLER_FINAL_56_traj                2009-Mar-07 04:57   2201-Feb-20 00:00\n*******************************************************************************\n\n\n*******************************************************************************\nEphemeris / API_USER Tue Oct 27 09:16:12 2026 Pasadena, USA      / Horizons\n*******************************************************************************\nTarget body name: Kepler (spacecraft) (-227)      {source: KEPLER_FINAL_56_traj}\nCenter body name: Earth (399)                     {source: DE441}\nCenter-site name: BODY CENTER\n*******************************************************************************\nStart time      : A.D. 2009-Mar-07 04:56:36.0000 UT \nStop  time      : A.D. 2009-Mar-08 03:49:57.0000 UT \nStep-size       : 10 minutes\n*******************************************************************************\nCenter geodetic : 0.0, 0.0, 0.0                   {E-lon(deg),Lat(deg),Alt(km)}\nCenter cylindric: 0.0, 0.0, 0.0                   {E-lon(deg),Dxy(km),Dz(km)}\nCenter radii    : 6378.137, 6378.137, 6356.752 km {Equator_a, b, pole_c}\nOutput units    : KM-S\nCalendar mode   : Mixed Julian/Gregorian\nOutput type     : GEOMETRIC cartesian states\nOutput format   : 2 (position and velocity)\nEOP file        : eop.261026.p270122\nEOP coverage    : DATA-BASED 1962-JAN-20 TO 2026-OCT-26. PREDICTS-> 2027-JAN-21\nReference frame : ICRF\n*******************************************************************************\n            JDUT ,            Calendar Date (UT ),                